import { CollectionTransformCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> CollectionTransformCopy = CollectionTransformCopy {
  summary: "items.flatMap produces an Array from each element, then joins those Arrays by one level. It preserves input order and the order within each returned Array.",
  taskTitle: "Collect each group’s tasks into one list",
  purpose: "Extract tasks from the Today, Waiting, and Tomorrow groups. Waiting has no tasks. Join the other tasks in order and display Read, Review, Ship.",
  typescriptBefore: "groups.flatMap(group => group.tasks) extracts each object’s tasks and joins them by one level. An empty tasks array adds nothing. TypeScript combines mapping and flattening into this single call.",
  benefit: "Seseragi also accepts one function describing what to extract. It joins the elements of the returned Array<String> values, so the result is one Array<String> rather than Array<Array<String>>.",
  setup: "items is the alias for std/array. struct Group defines a record type with title: String and tasks: Array<String>, and Group { ... } constructs a value. let groups names an Array containing three Group records.",
  after: [
    "\\group -> group.tasks is an unnamed function that receives one group and returns its tasks field. The \\ starts the argument and the expression after -> is the returned value. Pass this function first and groups second to items.flatMap.",
    "Today supplies [\"Read\", \"Review\"], Waiting supplies [], and Tomorrow supplies [\"Ship\"]. The results are joined in that order. Waiting is still passed to the function, but its empty returned Array adds no elements.",
    "The comprehension [value | value <- ...] reads each element of the flatMap result as value and creates a display Array. The output is [\"Read\",\"Review\",\"Ship\"]; the groups’ title fields are not included.",
  ],
  details: [
    "The function is applied once to each Group from the beginning. The elements of each result keep their order, and neither groups nor its tasks are changed. An empty groups collection calls no function and returns an empty Array. This example’s function only reads a field and performs no external operations.",
    "Only one level of each returned Array is joined. If two inputs produce [[1], [11]] and [[2], [12]], the result is [[1], [11], [2], [12]]. The deeper Arrays remain intact.",
    "The function must return an Array. Returning only the String group.title does not fit. Return [group.title] to contribute one title, and distinguish this from a function returning a List.",
    "Use concat when you already have Array<Array<String>> and need no per-element transformation. Use filterMap when each input contributes at most one value and you want to express absence with Nothing.",
  ],
}
